new-page: true

= Choose your difficulty

The year is 1917. The Tsar is gone, the war is not, and Russia is in the hands of a dozen competing authorities. You lead the Mensheviks: the Russian Social Democrats, and for now the largest party of the Petrograd Soviet.

- @easy: Easy. More resources, a bigger hand, and cards can be returned.
- @normal: Normal.
- @hard: Hard. Fewer resources, factions that are harder to hold together, and no returning cards.

@easy
on-arrival: difficulty = -1; resources = 5; dues = 3; hand_size = 4; intl_dissent = 10; rightdef_dissent = 5;
go-to: intro

@normal
on-arrival: difficulty = 0;
go-to: intro

@hard
on-arrival: difficulty = 1; resources = 1; dues = 1; discard_disabled = 1; intl_dissent = 30; rightdef_dissent = 20; unions_dissent = 15; rel_sr = 55;
go-to: intro

@intro
new-page: true
go-to: post_event

= March 1917

> *"The Russian revolution is a bourgeois revolution; it can only be a bourgeois revolution, and the democratic bourgeoisie must lead it."*
>
> The Menshevik reading of 1917 (paraphrased)

The February Revolution has overthrown the monarchy. A Provisional Government of liberals sits in the Tauride Palace, and in the same building a Soviet of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies, led by Mensheviks and Socialist Revolutionaries, holds the real power: the garrison, the railways and the telegraph obey the Soviet, not the ministers.

Your party has an unusual task. It is Russia's social democracy, in the same International as the German SPD, and it believes that Russia is not ready for socialism. It wants a democratic republic, the end of the war on fair terms, and room for workers to organise. Its rivals say that this is the road to disaster.

Every two weeks you will play a card: a meeting, a campaign, a ministry, an advisor's advice. Between cards, history happens. You lead the party until December 1921. You can lose it to a counter-revolution of the Right, to a Bolshevik one-party state, or to exile. Or you can save something of what was won in February.

*This is a game of alternate history. The dates are given in the New Style (Gregorian) calendar; before February 1918 Russia kept the Old Style, thirteen days behind.*

*Red October is a work in progress.*
